## How the CGP examples are built

A CGP project is organized as a template:

- `CMakeLists.txt`: the build of the project;
- `cgp/`: the library;
- `src/`: your scene (this is where you usually edit the code);
- `shaders/`: the shaders.

The examples of this tutorial only contain the files that differ from the template. The build script copies
the template, places the files of the example over it, adds the bridge, and compiles with the
`CMakeLists.txt` of CGP itself:

```
build/<example>-stage/      a copy of the CGP project (CGP_DIR)
  src/                      the template, then the files of the example on top, and the bridge
  CMakeLists.txt            the one of CGP, plus the link options of the bridge
```

This first CGP example has no file of its own: it is the template as it is.

- **&gt;** With the CGP project in a folder, and the Emscripten SDK activated, run from the root of the repository:

  ```
  CGP_DIR=<the CGP project> python3 scripts/build_examples.py c1-cgp-template
  CGP_DIR=<the CGP project> python3 scripts/build_examples.py --native c1-cgp-template
  ```

  - The first command compiles the web version into `examples/c1-cgp-template/web/`, tests it in a browser, and
    captures its poster.
  - With `--native`, the script also compiles the desktop version, and runs it for two seconds.

In your own CGP project, the same can be done in three steps:

- **&gt;** Copy `slide_bridge.hpp` and `slide_bridge.cpp` into `src/`.
- **&gt;** Add the link options of the bridge to the web build in `CMakeLists.txt`.
- **&gt;** Use `slide-frame.html` as the page of the demo.

